    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111142\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n442 AM PDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances and \n  an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring gusty winds to the region over the \n  weekend into early next week.\n\n* Cooler than normal temperatures will stick around through much of \n  next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Saturday.\n\nSunday looks to be the highest impact day, with showers and \nthunderstorms becoming more widespread overnight and continuing \nthroughout the day on Sunday. Flash Flood Watches remain in effect \nfor most of the area through 5:00 AM PDT/MST on Monday. Evening \nsatellite imagery shows post-tropical cyclone Rachel drifting inland \nnear Ensenada on the Baja Peninsula. Rachel's remnant circulation \nwill continue to track northeast, moving into southern San \nBernardino County early Sunday morning, moving up the Colorado River \nValley throughout the morning and early afternoon before moving into \nsouthwestern Utah by late afternoon. As Rachel swings through, \nshowers and thunderstorms will become more widespread through the \nmorning and into the afternoon. Heavy rain, flash flooding, and \nstrong winds will be the primary threats with these storms. High \nresolution models show the potential for a swath of 1.0+ inch rain \ntotals extending across the higher terrain of San Bernardino, into \nDeath Valley, and southern Nevada by late morning. In addition to \nheavy rains, flash flooding, and strong winds, the Storm Prediction \nCenter (SPC) has highlighted the lower Colorado River Valley with a \n2% tornado risk. Should we see a tornado develop, it will be short \nlived and on the lower end of the EF-scale. As the remnants of \nRachel begin to exit our forecast area, w will see an upper-level \ntrough push into the area late Sunday morning/early Sunday \nafternoon. This trough combined with the lingering moisture from \nRachel, will help to fuel another round of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the region. This trough will remain over the area as we go \ninto next week, with chances for isolated-to-scattered convection \nlingering through Tuesday before things dry out Wednesday onward.\n\nAs Rachel's remnant circulation approaches and moves through the \narea, we will see strong southerly winds pick up across the Mojave \nDesert with wind gusts in the 40 to 55 mph range. A Wind Advisory \nwill go into effect at 2:00 AM on Sunday for San Bernardino, Clark, \nand Mohave Counties as Rachel begins to approach our forecast area. \nThis Wind Advisory and the Wind Advisory for the southern Great \nBasin and Death Valley will remain in effect through 5:00 am Monday \nmorning. As Rachel moves into up the Colorado River Valley, we \nexpect to see stronger winds mix down to the surface, with 60+ mph \nwind gusts possible in the Colorado River Valley, Mohave County, \nsouthern and Northeastern Clark County, and Lincoln County. As \nsuch, a High Wind Warning will go into effect for the \naforementioned areas beginning at 5:00 am Sunday morning and \nrunning through 8:00 pm Sunday evening. Breezy winds will linger \ninto early next week, but will not be as intense as winds on \nSunday.\n\nThe aforementioned trough, which will fuel additional storms on \nSunday, will linger over the area through Tuesday, before exiting \nthe region on Wednesday and Thursday. As a result of these low 500 \nmb heights, temperatures will cool each day through Tuesday, \ngradually warming back up during the second half of the week as 500 \nmb heights recover. Even with this late week warm up, temperatures \nwill remain cooler than normal as we head into next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package... \nScattered showers and embedded thunderstorms will continue through \nthe morning, with the heaviest precipitation expected through 20Z. \nThe threat of showers and storms will persist through the afternoon, \nbut the latest guidance indicates drier air will begin moving into \nthe valley around 00Z, reducing precipitation chances. Potential \nimpacts include heavy rainfall reducing visibilities to below 6SM \nand lowering CIGs to around 5kft to 7kft AGL. Winds through daybreak \nwill favor easterly directions, but southeast to south winds with \ngusts of 30 to 35 knots are likely from mid-morning through the \nafternoon.  Winds will diminish after sunset, although occasional \ngusts of 15 to 20 knots will still be possible through the night. \nAdditional showers are likely on Monday.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Ongoing shower and \nthunderstorm activity will continue across much of the region into \nthis afternoon.  The activity should diminish from southwest to \nnortheast late this afternoon and evening, although a few showers \nand storms will persist into tonight, mainly over the northeastern \nthird of the region. Potential impacts from the precipitation \ninclude heavy rainfall reducing visibilities to below 6SM and \nlowering CIGs to around 5kft to 7kft AGL. Winds will also increase \nand become gusty this morning, with the strongest winds occurring in \nthe Colorado River Valley where some gusts could exceed 45 knots at \ntimes late this morning and afternoon. Lighter winds are expected \novernight, although a few gusts to 20 knots will still be possible \nin the river valley. Additional showers and isolated storms are \nlikely across most of the region on Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman \nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage: \nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
